INT. COUNTY PAROLE OFFICE - WAITING AREA - MORNING

Plastic chairs bolted in rows. A water cooler gurgles. Behind the glass, a clerk types, never looking up.

Big Dave, 50s, broad, in a suit that fits like it was lent to him, sits with his hands folded. Beside him, Jen Park, 40s, parole officer, flips through a file with her thumb.

JEN
(to the room, not looking up)
Everybody sign the sheet. Even if you've signed it before. Especially if you've signed it before.

Two teenagers by the vending machine laugh at something on a phone. A man with a toddler on his knee shushes the child, who cries anyway.

BIG DAVE
(standing, to Jen)
I've been out eleven months. I've been on time every Tuesday. Every Tuesday, Jen.

JEN
(beat)
I know.

BIG DAVE
(quieter)
Then why am I looking at a new condition on my own paper?

JEN
(closing the file)
Dave. Sit.

BIG DAVE
(not sitting)
I did what you said. I took the job. I stayed at Mo's place. I stayed away from the old crowd.

Mo, 30s, in the next chair over with a hi-vis vest in her lap, stares at the floor.

MO
(muttering)
Don't drag me into this.

BIG DAVE
(to Mo)
You're the one who said they'd write the letter.

MO
(to nobody)
I said I'd think about it.

JEN
(to the whole room)
Folks, give us a minute. Please.

The two teenagers drift off down the hall. The man with the toddler picks up the child and leaves. The clerk behind the glass finally looks up.

BIG DAVE
(beat)
You want to tell me who made the call?

JEN
(firmly)
No.

BIG DAVE
(louder)
Somebody called. Somebody told you where I was on the fourteenth.

JEN
(rising)
Dave, I can't discuss it here.

BIG DAVE
(lunges across the chair)
Then where?

Jen and Big Dave collide and both stumble into the water cooler. The jug SLOSHES and tips. Mo jumps up and the three of them freeze.

MO
(very quietly)
It was me.

Silence. The cooler glugs once, and stops.

BIG DAVE
(beat)
Say that again.

MO
(to Jen)
You said if I told you, he would only get a warning.

JEN
(to herself)
God help me.

Big Dave sits. Slowly, like a man lowering something heavy. The clerk behind the glass quietly turns off her screen.
